1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,705
A fountain jet.

2
00:00:01,705 --> 00:00:02,907
A long jumper.

3
00:00:02,907 --> 00:00:04,374
A volleyball serve.

4
00:00:04,774 --> 00:00:09,252
All three rise, slow down, and
stop rising at one highest point.

5
00:00:09,702 --> 00:00:14,327
On the college ground, Aarav throws
a ball at fourteen metres per second,

6
00:00:14,327 --> 00:00:15,642
thirty degrees up.

7
00:00:15,642 --> 00:00:17,777
It rises two point five metres.

8
00:00:18,127 --> 00:00:22,405
His puzzle: if he throws it twice
as fast, will it go twice as high?

9
00:00:22,905 --> 00:00:24,280
Most people say yes.

10
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:27,337
By the end, you will see
why the answer is no.

11
00:00:27,987 --> 00:00:29,587
So what is maximum height?

12
00:00:29,936 --> 00:00:33,835
It is the greatest height a projectile
reaches, above the point where it

13
00:00:33,835 --> 00:00:34,735
was thrown.

14
00:00:35,026 --> 00:00:40,275
Split the launch velocity u into two
parts: u cos theta across, and u sine

15
00:00:40,275 --> 00:00:41,175
theta up.

16
00:00:41,563 --> 00:00:43,259
Gravity pulls only down.

17
00:00:43,259 --> 00:00:47,746
So it slows the vertical part, and
leaves the horizontal part alone.

18
00:00:48,146 --> 00:00:51,452
The ball keeps rising while
the vertical part points up.

19
00:00:51,452 --> 00:00:54,858
When it reaches zero, the
ball is at its highest point.

20
00:00:55,508 --> 00:01:00,076
Let's launch a ball at nineteen point
six metres per second, sixty degrees

21
00:01:00,076 --> 00:01:00,976
up.

22
00:01:00,885 --> 00:01:02,155
Watch the readout.

23
00:01:02,155 --> 00:01:06,128
The horizontal velocity stays at
nine point eight, all the way.

24
00:01:06,528 --> 00:01:10,946
The vertical velocity starts at about
seventeen, and drops by nine point

25
00:01:10,946 --> 00:01:12,310
eight every second.

26
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:17,112
On the graph, the line slopes down
and crosses zero at one point seven

27
00:01:17,112 --> 00:01:18,161
three seconds.

28
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:19,426
That is the top.

29
00:01:19,926 --> 00:01:21,274
Slow-motion replay.

30
00:01:21,274 --> 00:01:22,750
Freeze at the top.

31
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:25,987
The red arrow is g, still pointing down.

32
00:01:25,987 --> 00:01:27,891
The vertical arrow is gone.

33
00:01:27,891 --> 00:01:30,207
Only the horizontal arrow is left.

34
00:01:30,707 --> 00:01:32,023
Now the ghost copies.

35
00:01:32,023 --> 00:01:35,458
On the way up, the steps in
height get smaller and smaller.

36
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:38,899
The ball tops out at
fourteen point seven metres.

37
00:01:38,899 --> 00:01:42,767
On the graph, that height is
the area of the shaded triangle.

38
00:01:43,417 --> 00:01:44,330
Your turn.

39
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:48,510
Halfway to the top in time,
has the ball risen half of H?

40
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:49,854
Pause and decide.

41
00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:53,954
No!

42
00:01:53,524 --> 00:01:55,415
It rises fastest early on.

43
00:01:55,415 --> 00:01:59,043
After half the time, it is
already at eleven metres.

44
00:01:59,043 --> 00:02:01,086
Three quarters of the height.

45
00:02:01,736 --> 00:02:03,962
Now the formula, step by step.

46
00:02:04,312 --> 00:02:05,212
Step one.

47
00:02:05,198 --> 00:02:06,980
Look only up and down.

48
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:11,483
The ball starts up at u sine
theta, with acceleration minus g.

49
00:02:12,233 --> 00:02:13,133
Step two.

50
00:02:13,063 --> 00:02:16,594
At the highest point, the
vertical velocity is zero.

51
00:02:17,344 --> 00:02:18,365
Step three.

52
00:02:18,365 --> 00:02:21,750
Use v squared equals u
squared plus two a s.

53
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:26,212
So zero equals u squared sine
squared theta, minus two g H.

54
00:02:26,962 --> 00:02:27,891
Step four.

55
00:02:27,891 --> 00:02:31,803
So H equals u squared sine
squared theta, over two g.

56
00:02:31,803 --> 00:02:34,004
The units work out to metres.

57
00:02:34,754 --> 00:02:39,918
And from v equals u plus a t, the
time to reach the top is u sine theta

58
00:02:39,918 --> 00:02:40,818
over g.

59
00:02:41,446 --> 00:02:42,525
Remember this.

60
00:02:42,525 --> 00:02:45,793
At the top, only the
vertical velocity is zero.

61
00:02:45,793 --> 00:02:47,608
The ball is still moving.

62
00:02:48,308 --> 00:02:49,801
Now, Aarav's puzzle.

63
00:02:49,801 --> 00:02:51,466
Why not twice as high?

64
00:02:51,816 --> 00:02:56,481
Throw twice as fast, and the vertical
part, u sine theta, is also twice

65
00:02:56,481 --> 00:02:57,381
as big.

66
00:02:57,589 --> 00:03:02,063
Gravity removes nine point eight every
second, so it takes twice as long

67
00:03:02,063 --> 00:03:03,228
to stop rising.

68
00:03:03,628 --> 00:03:07,841
So the ball rises twice as fast
on average, for twice as long.

69
00:03:08,241 --> 00:03:09,791
Two times two is four.

70
00:03:09,791 --> 00:03:11,717
The height becomes four times.

71
00:03:11,717 --> 00:03:14,338
That is why u is squared in the formula.

72
00:03:14,988 --> 00:03:16,126
Side by side.

73
00:03:16,126 --> 00:03:19,585
Same speed, nineteen point
six metres per second.

74
00:03:19,585 --> 00:03:22,623
One ball at thirty degrees, one at sixty.

75
00:03:23,023 --> 00:03:26,404
At thirty degrees it rises
four point nine metres.

76
00:03:26,404 --> 00:03:29,138
At sixty degrees, fourteen point seven.

77
00:03:29,738 --> 00:03:30,795
Now you try.

78
00:03:30,795 --> 00:03:32,160
The angle doubled.

79
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,858
How many times higher did the ball go?

80
00:03:34,858 --> 00:03:36,172
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:39,372 --> 00:03:40,359
Three times!

82
00:03:40,359 --> 00:03:42,394
H follows sine squared theta.

83
00:03:42,394 --> 00:03:46,209
Three quarters divided by one
quarter is three, not two.

84
00:03:46,859 --> 00:03:50,423
And straight up, at ninety
degrees, sine theta is one.

85
00:03:50,423 --> 00:03:54,822
That gives the greatest height, u
squared over two g: nineteen point six

86
00:03:54,822 --> 00:03:55,722
metres.

87
00:03:56,049 --> 00:03:57,785
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:58,135 --> 00:03:59,230
Back to Aarav.

89
00:03:59,230 --> 00:04:03,262
He throws at twenty eight metres
per second, thirty degrees up.

90
00:04:03,262 --> 00:04:05,113
How high does the ball go?

91
00:04:05,613 --> 00:04:09,564
Use H equals u squared sine
squared theta, over two g.

92
00:04:10,314 --> 00:04:14,333
The vertical part is twenty eight
times sine thirty, which is fourteen

93
00:04:14,333 --> 00:04:15,557
metres per second.

94
00:04:16,307 --> 00:04:20,953
So H is fourteen squared, one
ninety six, over nineteen point six.

95
00:04:21,703 --> 00:04:23,192
That is ten metres.

96
00:04:23,192 --> 00:04:25,951
Four times his first throw, not twice.

97
00:04:26,601 --> 00:04:28,066
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:28,416 --> 00:04:32,703
Many students mix up the formulas,
and put sine two theta in the height.

99
00:04:33,053 --> 00:04:33,953
Wrong.

100
00:04:33,666 --> 00:04:36,147
Sine two theta belongs to the range.

101
00:04:36,147 --> 00:04:40,104
Height uses sine squared theta:
the vertical part, squared.

102
00:04:40,754 --> 00:04:42,197
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:42,197 --> 00:04:45,578
As numericals and ratios
on height, speed and angle.

104
00:04:46,178 --> 00:04:47,354
Exam level now.

105
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:51,918
At its highest point, a projectile's
speed is half its launch speed u.

106
00:04:51,918 --> 00:04:53,811
What is its maximum height?

107
00:04:53,811 --> 00:04:55,037
Pause and solve.

108
00:05:00,437 --> 00:05:01,863
The answer is B.

109
00:05:01,863 --> 00:05:04,140
Three u squared over eight g.

110
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:07,930
At the top, only the
horizontal part is left.

111
00:05:07,930 --> 00:05:10,423
So u cos theta equals u over two.

112
00:05:10,923 --> 00:05:15,778
So cos theta is one half, theta is
sixty degrees, and sine squared theta

113
00:05:15,778 --> 00:05:17,154
is three quarters.

114
00:05:17,654 --> 00:05:21,218
H equals u squared times
three quarters, over two g.

115
00:05:21,218 --> 00:05:23,808
That is three u squared over eight g.

116
00:05:24,408 --> 00:05:25,869
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:25,869 --> 00:05:27,370
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:30,570 --> 00:05:33,568
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:33,968 --> 00:05:34,868
One.

120
00:05:34,456 --> 00:05:38,252
At the very top of the flight,
what is the ball's velocity?

121
00:05:39,152 --> 00:05:40,052
Two.

122
00:05:39,649 --> 00:05:44,065
If the launch speed triples, how
many times higher does the ball go?

123
00:05:44,965 --> 00:05:45,865
Three.

124
00:05:45,549 --> 00:05:49,589
Why does the height use sine
squared theta, not sine two theta?

125
00:05:50,689 --> 00:05:54,059
Next time: ninety degrees
gives the greatest height.

126
00:05:54,059 --> 00:05:56,939
But which angle sends
the ball the farthest?

127
00:05:56,939 --> 00:05:59,110
We find out in horizontal range.
